8.0	DESIGN OF EXPERIMENTS
8.1	Experimental Design
This study has two parts:
8.1.1	Part 1 is a transit simulation, conducted according to ISTA 3A by Unified2 Global Packaging Group. This test is appropriate for single packages up to 70 kg (150 lb) for distribution by air or ground, from pick up at the manufacturer’s site to delivery at the destination. The transportation cycle will be simulated using the method defined in ISTA 3A, following the sequence listed in Table 4 – Test Sequence and Levels.
Table 2 - Test Sequence and Levels
Sequence Number	Test Category	Test Type	Test Level
1	Atmospheric Preconditioning 
TEST BLOCK 1	Temperature and Humidity	Ambient
3	Shock
TEST BLOCK 3	Drop (not in a bag)	9 Drops – height varies with packaged-product weight
4	Vibration
TEST BLOCKS 6 & 7	Random With and Without Top Load	Overall Grms level of 0.53 <and 0.46>
5	Vibration
TEST BLOCKS 2 & 8	Random Vibration Under Low Pressure	Truck or Truck & Air Dependent (Optional)
6	Shock
TEST BLOCK 9	Drop (in a bag)	7 Drops – height varies with packaged-product weight
8.1.2	Part 2 consists of a functional evaluation to determine if the conditions in Part 1 had an adverse effect on the functionality of the ADS Injector. This test will be conducted by NXP.

8.2	Sample Size Rationale
Sample size has been established per VVP-0961-20000. The Type Acceptance Test (TAT) follows the internationally recognized performance test procedure ISTA 3A. This procedure specifies a sample size of one for transit simulation. One test article is adequate for this evaluation because the intent is to evaluate whether the test articles, when designed and manufactured and processed according to approved specifications for clinical units, satisfy the requirements of the Product Performance Characteristics document. Per customer needs, 8 devices will be tested.
		
8.3	Data Analysis
A functional verification test, per TM-0961-20000, will be used to determine if the test product was not damaged during Part 1. This study is being conducted as a Type Acceptance Test and the results are self-evident. Therefore, specification of a statistical method for data analysis is not required.
8.4	Deviations and Discrepancies
8.4.1	Deviations
Deviations to this protocol are permitted, provided they are reviewed by the protocol approvers.
Deviations will be documented in the report, stating what was supposed to occur, what actually did occur, and justification for the effect on the study.
8.4.2	Discrepancies
Discrepancies will be reviewed on a case-by-case basis to determine the effect on the study. In order to accept a discrepant result as passing, the review must demonstrate that the design intent was met.
Discrepancies will be documented in the report, stating what was supposed to occur, what actually did occur, and justification for the effect on the study.

9.0	ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA
The results of this study will be evaluated against these criteria. All criteria must be met in order for the study to be considered successful.
Table 3 - Acceptance Criteria
Criterion	Specification	Acceptance
1	The shipping container must have:
•	Legible labels.
•	Scanner-readable bar codes.	All samples meet specification.
2	The items contained within the interior shall reside in their proper position, with no items missing.	All samples meet specification.
3	The exterior of each device will be free of visible damage with the unaided eye, including but not limited to, dents, cracks, loose/missing components, loose/missing screws, misaligned plugs (if appropriate), and broken switches (if appropriate), paint abrasion, scratches, etc.	All samples meet specification.
4	All test articles must meet applicable functional requirements.	All samples meet specification.
10.0	NON-CONFORMANCES
10.1	Any injector, subassembly component that does not meet the criteria above will be investigated to identify the root cause, and if not directly associated to the design or the packaging, the team will consider if it is appropriate to consider it a pass or failure.
